# The site address comes from HOST_URL, so the domain is configured in one place and the
# backend's CORS origin, the OpenAPI `servers` entry and the certificate always agree.
{$HOST_URL} {
	encode zstd gzip

	# ── The gate ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
	#
	# The whole site is behind one shared password. This deployment is a closed test that people
	# are let into one at a time, and this block is what makes that true rather than merely
	# intended.
	#
	# **Here rather than in the application**, because it has to cover everything: the API, the
	# OpenAPI document, the uploaded files, and any route added later without anybody thinking
	# about this. An application-level gate covers what it was told to cover.
	#
	# It comes down when the site opens to the public, together with the Impressum and the
	# Datenschutzerklärung that go live with it: delete this `route` wrapper — the two `handle`
	# blocks move back out one level — and the `GATE_*` variables from `.env`.
	#
	# Wrapped in `route` so the order is the order written here. Caddy sorts directives by its
	# own table otherwise, and "does the password come before or after the routing" is not a
	# question worth being unsure about on the thing that decides who reaches the site at all.
	route {
		# ── The interim mailbox ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
		#
		# Mailpit, while there is no SMTP account: nothing the platform sends reaches a real
		# inbox, and this is where the team fetches a confirmation link for somebody.
		#
		# **Its own password, and never the site's.** Every password-reset link in the platform
		# passes through here, so whoever can read this mailbox can take over any account. The
		# site password is meant to be handed to a group of testers; this one is not.
		#
		# First in the `route`, so it answers before the site gate does. Two `basic_auth`
		# blocks in sequence could never both be satisfied — a request carries one
		# Authorization header, and it cannot be two different credentials at once.
		handle /{$MAILPIT_WEBROOT:postfach}* {
			# Its own realm. A browser files credentials under origin *and* realm, and both gates
			# defaulted to "restricted" — so the site's password and the mailbox's landed in the
			# same drawer on the same domain, and each would be offered where the other belongs.
			basic_auth bcrypt "Rollenspielhimmel Postfach" {
				{$MAILBOX_USERNAME} {$MAILBOX_PASSWORD_HASH}
			}
			reverse_proxy mailpit:8025
		}

		# ── What the browser fetches of its own accord ──────────────────────────────────────
		#
		# Icons and the manifest, before the gate rather than behind it.
		#
		# **Because a browser fetches these without being asked, and without credentials.** A web
		# app manifest is fetched with credentials omitted unless the tag says otherwise; an
		# apple-touch-icon is fetched by iOS on its own terms entirely. Behind the gate each of
		# them got a 401 — and a 401 on a subresource is a second password prompt. Measured on a
		# laptop: the manifest first, and once that was fixed by an attribute, the icon took its
		# place. Attribute by attribute is a losing game, because the next browser fetches the
		# next thing its own way.
		#
		# **Nothing here is secret**, which is the whole reason this is allowed: five icons and a
		# file naming the site and two colours. A visitor without the password learns that a site
		# called Rollenspielhimmel exists — which the domain and the public certificate logs say
		# already. The pages, the API and everything anybody wrote stay behind the gate.
		#
		# `apple-touch-icon-precomposed.png` and `robots.txt` do not exist. They are listed
		# anyway: some iOS versions probe the first name unprompted, and a `robots.txt` has to be
		# reachable to mean anything the day the gate comes down. Missing files answer 404 here
		# rather than falling through to the application's entry point.
		#
		# Exact paths, never a prefix: a wildcard is how something that was meant to stay behind
		# a gate ends up in front of one.
		@unbewacht path /favicon.ico /favicon.svg /apple-touch-icon.png /apple-touch-icon-precomposed.png /icon-192.png /icon-512.png /site.webmanifest /robots.txt
		handle @unbewacht {
			root * /srv

			header {
				# The one header that matters for a file served without the block below: these
				# are images and JSON, and none of them should ever be sniffed into a script.
				X-Content-Type-Options nosniff

				# A day. Unlike `/assets`, these names carry no content hash, so a changed icon
				# has to be able to arrive without a new name.
				Cache-Control "public, max-age=86400"
			}

			file_server
		}

		# ── The pass ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
		#
		# Whoever already carries the pass is not asked again.
		#
		# **Why this exists at all.** HTTP basic authentication has no cookie and no lifetime: a
		# browser keeps the credentials in memory, per origin and realm, and drops them when the
		# session ends. Safari on iOS does that eagerly — a new tab, the app backgrounded, the
		# phone locked. „Ask once and then be quiet" is not something the mechanism can do, so
		# this adds the piece it lacks.
		#
		# **What it costs, stated plainly rather than glossed over.** Before, somebody had to
		# know the password. Now, access to a device that holds the pass is enough. That is a
		# real reduction, and it was decided knowingly: this gate stands in front of a site that
		# has a real sign-in behind it, and it exists to keep a half-finished beta out of
		# strangers' hands, not to protect what is inside.
		#
		# **Revocable, which is the point of taking the value from `.env`.** Changing the
		# password would not help — the pass goes around it. Changing `GATE_COOKIE_VALUE` and
		# redeploying invalidates every pass at once, on every device, this one included. That is
		# exactly what is wanted the day one has to be taken back.
		#
		# The value must be alphanumeric: it is interpolated into a regular expression, and a
		# character with a meaning there would quietly change what matches. `.example.deploy.env`
		# says so, and the compose file refuses to start without a value — an empty one would
		# make this match a cookie of `einlass=` and let anybody in.
		@ohne_einlass {
			not header_regexp Cookie "(?:^|;\s*)einlass={$GATE_COOKIE_VALUE}(?:;|$)"
		}

		# Both interpolated from `.env`. An empty hash makes Caddy refuse to start, which fails
		# the deploy rather than quietly serving the site to everybody — the same direction
		# `ENVIRONMENT` fails in, and for the same reason.
		basic_auth @ohne_einlass bcrypt "Rollenspielhimmel" {
			{$GATE_USERNAME} {$GATE_PASSWORD_HASH}
		}

		# Handed out on the way back, so only a request that got past the line above can carry
		# one away. A year, because the question this answers is „how often does a tester have to
		# type this", and the answer should be „once".
		#
		# `HttpOnly` so no script can read it, `Secure` so it never leaves over plain HTTP, and
		# `SameSite=Lax` so another site cannot make a browser spend it.
		#
		# Not on the API and not on the fingerprinted files: those are answered in their hundreds
		# and would repeat the same header every time. The document is what a person arrives at,
		# and it is enough.
		@einlass_mitgeben {
			not path /api/* /assets/*
		}
		header @einlass_mitgeben +Set-Cookie "einlass={$GATE_COOKIE_VALUE}; Path=/; Max-Age=31536000; Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax"

		# Everything the backend serves lives under one prefix, so adding a resource needs no
		# change here and no route can be forgotten.
		handle /api/* {
			reverse_proxy backend:8000 {
				# Caddy buffers by default, which holds an endless text/event-stream forever.
				# Works locally without it, so this is unverifiable before deploying.
				flush_interval -1

				# Holds a request across a backend restart rather than answering 502; only a
				# failed dial is retried. Nothing when Caddy itself is down, which the interface
				# handles.
				lb_try_duration 5s

				# The limiter buckets by the first X-Forwarded-For entry, so this must replace
				# what the client sent, not append. Caddy's default does; not left to it.
				header_up X-Forwarded-For {remote_host}
			}
		}

		# The single page application. Unknown paths fall back to its entry point, so a
		# client-side route survives a reload.
		handle {
			root * /srv

			# `secureHeaders` never runs for static files, so without this the document loading
			# all the JavaScript would be the only unprotected response. Kept in step with it.
			header {
				Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=15552000; includeSubDomains"
				X-Content-Type-Options nosniff
				X-Frame-Options SAMEORIGIN
				Referrer-Policy no-referrer
				Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy same-origin
				Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy same-origin

				# Inline styles because the dropdown positions itself through the attribute;
				# scripts stay strict. `blob:` is the upload preview. No third party is named.
				Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; font-src 'self'; img-src 'self' data: blob:; connect-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'"
			}

			# Everything under /assets is content-hashed, so a name cannot change meaning and a
			# year is safe. The entry point stays uncached, which is what lets a deploy land.
			@fingerprinted path /assets/*
			header @fingerprinted Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable"

			try_files {path} /index.html
			file_server
		}
	}
}
